    <edge source="&quot;TOKITSUKAZE&quot;" target="&quot;VAL(A)&quot;">
      <data key="d3">19.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Tokitsukaze is the originator of the problem whose central quantity is val(a), the sum of absolute differences between consecutive sequence elements."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tokitsukaze is the person who defines and seeks to compute the expected value of val(a), making them directly associated with this mathematical event."</data>
      <data key="d5">chunk-2a6cf2c1b884640bd458e20b236729e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-643a1dae351fde67dd0b6b4bafe4269b</data>
      <data key="d6">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;TOKITSUKAZE&quot;" target="&quot;L[I]&quot;">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Tokitsukaze's problem defines L[i] as the lower bound for the random generation of sequence elements, a key input to the expectation calculation."</data>
      <data key="d5">chunk-f207867e95527d1c21eb90640d585ef3</data>
      <data key="d6">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;TOKITSUKAZE&quot;" target="&quot;R[I]&quot;">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Tokitsukaze's problem defines R[i] as the upper bound for the random generation of sequence elements, a key input to the expectation calculation."</data>
      <data key="d5">chunk-f207867e95527d1c21eb90640d585ef3</data>
      <data key="d6">1</data>
    </edge>
